WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:02.670
CARTER MORGAN: Welcome to Cloud
Security Basics, a new series

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:04.950
where we explain the
ins and outs of securing

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:06.880
your application
on Google Cloud.

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:07.440
Sound fun?

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.270
Then stick around,
because in this episode,

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:12.120
you'll learn about the second
of three distinct areas of cloud

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:13.410
security risk--

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:14.019
data.

00:00:14.019 --> 00:00:17.762
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:31.734
[DIAL TONE]

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:34.450
BRAD ACTOR: Hello,
cloud detective.

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:37.030
You may have found protections
for the first cloud security

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:38.260
risk-- access.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:40.860
But how much have you thought
about the second risk--

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:42.320
data?

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:45.820
How can you prevent problems
with the data you store?

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:48.760
There are many ways
to get trapped here.

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:52.130
Improper disclosure of
your info by employees.

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:54.910
Imagine accidentally sending
an email containing credit card

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:57.550
or social security numbers.

00:00:57.550 --> 00:01:00.370
Your application could
disseminate harmful data,

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:03.160
sharing phishing
URLs or malware.

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:05.349
Or, data could be
stored somewhere

00:01:05.349 --> 00:01:08.300
without proper security
protocols in place.

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:10.600
What can you do to stop that?

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:12.230
For your data's sake,
I hope, you know,

00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:15.172
the answer to my question.

00:01:15.172 --> 00:01:16.630
CARTER MORGAN:
Here's what we know.

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:20.038
Data represents what data gets
stored and where it's located.

00:01:20.038 --> 00:01:21.580
You must be sure
that you don't store

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:24.700
personally identifiable
information, or PII, which

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:27.640
is data that could potentially
be used to identify a user.

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:30.100
And even if you don't store
sensitive information,

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:32.050
if you can't find
where you stored it,

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:33.717
that's not gong to
be very useful to you

00:01:33.717 --> 00:01:34.900
or your customers.

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:38.230
Typical concerns here include
something bad with the people

00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:40.310
who can access
machines-- for example,

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:43.570
a compromised employee
accessing data they shouldn't.

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:46.600
Something bad with the data
itself, like receiving bad data

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:48.700
or storing illegal data.

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:50.560
If the data coming into
your system is junk,

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:52.930
then even if every other
component of data security

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:54.910
is working as normal,
the system still

00:01:54.910 --> 00:01:56.350
won't function as intended.

00:01:56.350 --> 00:01:58.090
Sometimes unsanitized
data can even

00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:02.540
be used to exploit a system to
further compromise application.

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:05.310
Something bad with the
transfer or storage of data,

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:08.870
for example, not being able
to find where data is stored.

00:02:08.870 --> 00:02:11.120
In this case, the Cloud
solution would be losing data,

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:12.920
whether because it
never got stored,

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:15.223
or because it got
lost once it was.

00:02:15.223 --> 00:02:17.390
Either way, the end result
is that the data you need

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:19.520
can't be retrieved.

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:21.290
Something bad with
the machine data

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:23.780
is being stored on, like
it containing malware,

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:25.550
or if there were
compromised VMs.

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:27.950
In this case, even though
the data is stored correctly,

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:30.770
it might be leaked or
accessed by the wrong people.

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:34.070
Luckily, Google Cloud provides
a lot of tools and services

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:38.660
to protect its users, including
but not limited to IAM

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:41.360
for controlling access
to data resources,

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:43.580
encryption to make sure
stored and transferred

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:46.700
data can't be read,
even if it is stolen,

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:49.520
logging and monitoring to make
sure that you can track what's

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:51.740
happening in your system.

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:54.500
DeID for making sure PII
information is stripped

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:56.960
before it's ever
stored in your system,

00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:58.670
organizational
policy to set rules

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:02.050
on who can access what
data and where it's stored,

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:04.730
and better VMs or VM management,
like for SETI or Google

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:06.710
Host Patching, et
cetera, to make sure

00:03:06.710 --> 00:03:09.270
that the machines
you use are secure.

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:11.950
Let's see how they work.

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:14.880
So how do we prevent
problems with data we store?

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:16.620
I just listed a few tools.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:20.120
I want to highlight
a few of them.

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:22.940
Cloud IAM lets you
grant granular access

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:24.920
to specific Google
Cloud resources

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:27.710
and helps prevent access
to other resources.

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:30.170
Cloud IAM lets you adopt
the security principle

00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:32.450
of least privileged,
where you only

00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:35.993
grant necessary permissions
to access specific resources.

00:03:35.993 --> 00:03:37.910
This works to prevent
people from accidentally

00:03:37.910 --> 00:03:40.340
or maliciously accessing
data they shouldn't.

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:42.980
For example, IAM lets
you keep UI developers

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:45.520
from accessing credit card
databases they didn't need

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:47.180
to access in the first place.

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:49.550
Say there was an access
violation, though.

00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:51.110
That's where logging
and monitoring

00:03:51.110 --> 00:03:53.440
can be used to track
who is accessing what

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:56.690
and to automate the information
gathering and alerting process

00:03:56.690 --> 00:03:57.920
as much as possible.

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:01.130
Cloud platform provides tools,
such as Google Cloud Logging

00:04:01.130 --> 00:04:02.780
and Google Cloud
Monitoring, that

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:05.210
make it easy to collect
and analyze request logs

00:04:05.210 --> 00:04:08.420
and monitor the availability of
your infrastructure services,

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:10.370
like VM instances.

00:04:10.370 --> 00:04:13.100
These tools make it easy for
you to create custom dashboards

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:14.880
and set alerts
when issues occur.

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:16.790
For example, you could
set up notifications

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:19.339
to warn you of suspicious
activity in your system,

00:04:19.339 --> 00:04:21.350
or to notify you when
access to a resource

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:23.450
is taking too
long, for instance.

00:04:23.450 --> 00:04:26.060
But really, it's good to
be proactive as much as

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:29.040
possible and set up
organizational policy.

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:32.060
An organizational policy is
a centralized configuration

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:34.610
of restrictions on how your
organization's resources can

00:04:34.610 --> 00:04:35.720
be used.

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:37.940
They define guardrails
for your development teams

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:39.770
to stay within
compliance boundaries

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:41.870
and help teams move
quickly without worrying

00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:43.190
of breaking compliance.

00:04:43.190 --> 00:04:44.930
For example, you
could set a policy

00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:47.000
to require all newly
created, restarted,

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:49.880
or updated Cloud SQL
instances to use customer

00:04:49.880 --> 00:04:51.560
managed encryption keys.

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:56.910
All of these tools and more
work together to protect data.

00:04:56.910 --> 00:04:57.570
And that's it!

00:04:57.570 --> 00:05:01.410
I know the answer to
Brad Actor's question.

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:02.700
BRAD ACTOR: Hello, detective.

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:04.150
I see you've been busy.

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:05.700
You answered my little question.

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:08.520
What if there's a problem
with the data you store?

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:10.710
You learned that Google
provides a plethora of tools

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:14.760
to help customers protect data,
including IAM, encryption,

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:17.160
logging and monitoring,
organizational policy,

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:18.558
and more.

00:05:18.558 --> 00:05:20.100
These features allow
you to make sure

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:22.313
only valid data is
being stored, control

00:05:22.313 --> 00:05:23.730
who can access
data that's stored,

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:26.400
and help you find the
data you do store.

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:28.505
Very good, very good.

00:05:28.505 --> 00:05:29.880
You've answered
today's question.

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:32.220
But be ready,
because next time, I

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:34.110
want to see what you
know about securing

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:35.993
your virtual and
physical hardware.

00:05:35.993 --> 00:05:38.160
CARTER MORGAN: So there you
have it, another episode

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:39.900
of Cloud Security Basics.

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:43.080
Next episode, we'll focus on
the last of three distinct areas

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:44.430
of Cloud security risk--

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:45.600
platform.

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:47.670
In the meantime, if you
want to take a deeper

00:05:47.670 --> 00:05:49.343
dive into securing
data in the Cloud,

00:05:49.343 --> 00:05:51.510
then check out the article
linked in the description

00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:52.038
below.

00:05:52.038 --> 00:05:54.330
And stay tuned for the rest
of the Cloud Security Basic

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:56.980
series, because when
it comes to security,

00:05:56.980 --> 00:05:58.410
you can't let bad actors win.

00:05:58.410 --> 00:06:02.060
